  <fragment slot="heading">
    <h1>Calorie Counting: A Beginner Guide to Managing Your Diet</h1>
  </fragment>

  <h2>What Is Calorie Counting?</h2>
  <p>Calorie counting is the practice of tracking the energy content of the food and drinks you consume. By understanding how many calories you need versus how many you consume, you can manage your weight effectively — whether your goal is to lose, maintain, or gain weight.</p>

  <h2>Understanding Your Calorie Needs</h2>
  <p>Your total daily energy expenditure (TDEE) consists of several components:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Basal Metabolic Rate (BMR):</strong> Calories burned at rest for basic functions like breathing and circulation.</li>
    <li><strong>Physical activity:</strong> Calories burned through exercise and movement.</li>
    <li><strong>Thermic effect of food:</strong> Calories burned digesting and processing food (about 10% of total).</li>
  </ul>

  <p>Use our <a href="/calorie-calculator/">Calorie Calculator</a> to find your daily needs based on your age, height, weight, and activity level.</p>

  <h2>Calorie Guidelines by Goal</h2>
  <table class="comparison-table">
    <thead>
      <tr>
        <th>Goal</th>
        <th>Calorie Adjustment</th>
        <th>Weekly Change</th>
      </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>Weight loss</td><td>TDEE — 300 to 500 calories</td><td>0.5-1 lb (0.2-0.5 kg)</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Weight maintenance</td><td>Eat at TDEE</td><td>No change</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Weight gain (muscle)</td><td>TDEE + 200 to 300 calories</td><td>0.25-0.5 lb (0.1-0.2 kg)</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <h2>Macronutrients Explained</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Protein:</strong> 4 calories per gram. Essential for muscle repair and satiety. Aim for 20-30% of daily calories.</li>
    <li><strong>Carbohydrates:</strong> 4 calories per gram. Your body's primary energy source. Aim for 45-55% of daily calories.</li>
    <li><strong>Fats:</strong> 9 calories per gram. Important for hormone function and nutrient absorption. Aim for 20-30% of daily calories.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Tips for Successful Calorie Tracking</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Use a food scale</strong> for accuracy — estimates are often wrong.</li>
    <li><strong>Track everything</strong> including cooking oils, sauces, and drinks.</li>
    <li><strong>Be consistent</strong> — track the same way every day for best results.</li>
    <li><strong>Don't stress about perfection</strong> — consistency over time matters more than daily accuracy.</li>
    <li><strong>Reassess regularly</strong> — your calorie needs change as your weight changes.</li>
  </ul>

  <p>Start tracking with our free <a href="/calorie-calculator/">Calorie Calculator</a>.</p>
<h2>Understanding Calorie Deficit and Surplus</h2>
  <p>Weight change is fundamentally about energy balance. To lose weight, you need a calorie deficit consuming fewer calories than your body burns. A deficit of 500 calories per day typically leads to about one pound of weight loss per week. For weight gain, a calorie surplus of 300-500 calories per day supports muscle growth when combined with resistance training.</p>
  <p>Rather than extreme calorie restriction, aim for a moderate deficit of 300-500 calories per day. This produces sustainable weight loss of 1-2 pounds per week without triggering starvation responses that slow your metabolism. Very low-calorie diets are difficult to maintain and often lead to rapid weight regain.</p>
  <h2>Quality Matters: Nutrient-Dense Foods</h2>
  <p>Not all calories are equal when it comes to satiety and nutrition. Two hundred calories from vegetables, lean protein, and healthy fats will keep you full and provide essential nutrients. The same 200 calories from processed snacks will leave you hungry and lacking nutrition. Prioritize whole foods, lean proteins, fiber-rich vegetables, and healthy fats.</p>
  <p>Protein is particularly important when in a calorie deficit because it helps preserve muscle mass. Aim for 1.6 to 2.2 grams of protein per kilogram of body weight. Fiber-rich foods like vegetables, legumes, and whole grains increase fullness and improve digestive health.</p>
  <h2>Related Tools</h2>
  <p>Calculate your daily needs with our <a href="/calorie-calculator/">Calorie Calculator</a>. Track nutrition in recipes with the <a href="/recipe-nutrition-calculator/">Recipe Nutrition Calculator</a>. Monitor your overall health with the <a href="/bmi-calculator/">BMI Calculator</a>.</p>

  <h2>Key Takeaways</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>A moderate calorie deficit of 300-500 calories per day produces sustainable weight loss.</li>
    <li>Focus on nutrient-dense foods that provide more satiety and nutrition per calorie.</li>
    <li>Adequate protein intake is essential for preserving muscle mass during weight loss.</li>
    <li>Sleep quality, stress management, and hydration all play important roles in weight management.</li>
  </ul>
